Jean Beaufret.

The letter is the canonical French post-war opening of the Beaufret–Heidegger correspondence and the text that provoked Heidegger’s post-war reply inviting the correspondent to come to him.

The English excerpt reproduces the French phrase-cluster that is repeatedly cited in the Beaufret–Heidegger edition:

*Je croyais qu’un homme capable d’écrire Être et Temps… pouvait comprendre quelque chose aux hommes* – “I believed that a man who can write Being and Time could understand something about men”;

*… la métaphysique enfermerait un esprit en elle-même, confondant intimité avec hermétisme…* – “metaphysics enclosed a spirit within itself, confusing intimacy with hermeticism. On the contrary, intimacy offers a more delicate possibility of engaging with others without losing oneself in them”;

*Après tout cela, je m’en vais aussi triste et tourmenté que je suis arrivé, avec la douleur cachée de m’être trompé à un moment décisif de ma vie* – “After all this, I leave as sad and tormented as I arrived, with the hidden pain of having been mistaken at a decisive moment in my life. But believe me, I am not leaving angry”;

*… l’ultime solitude est la seule façon d’être proche des autres* – “ultimate solitude is the only way to be close to others”;

*Permettez-moi de dissiper toute méprise/équivoque… en vous appelant un ami. Vous resterez un ami pour moi dans la solitude secrète / secrète solitude de mon existence* – “Allow me to dispel any possible misunderstanding regarding this letter by calling you a friend. You will remain a friend to me in the secret solitude of my existence. And if you ever remember me, forget this episode… and always believe in my exceptional friendship”;

*… la valeur d’une existence ne se mesure pas à la hauteur de ses réalisations, mais à la profondeur de ses racines* – “The worth of an existence is not measured by the height of its achievements, but by the depth of its roots.”

That is Beaufret 1901-1982, naval officer, hellenist and first systematic French interpreter/translator of *Sein und Zeit*, who wrote Heidegger in summer/autumn 1945, usually dated 15/19/20/26 July 1945 with the autumn 1945-46 follow-up and later redactional reworkings in 1948. The letter is a wounded but non-accusatory farewell reproaching the author of *Being and Time* for failing to understand men and for turning intimacy into hermetic enclosure, while deliberately preserving an exceptional friendship in secret solitude.

Heidegger’s reply is the documented post-war reconciliation: breaking his isolation and inviting Beaufret to come to him, realised in the stays at the Hütte in Todtnauberg in summer 1946, 1947/48, 1949 and the *Entretiens de Todtnauberg*, i.e. the 1945-1970 Beaufret–Heidegger correspondence that made Beaufret Heidegger’s principal French gate-keeper.

The Sartre 1933-36 Freiburg admiration-disappointment letters remain the acknowledged literary model on which Beaufret consciously built his 1945 text, which explains the routine conflation. It does not displace authorship: the post-war chronology, the French intimacy/hermeticism register, the secret solitude/exceptional friendship wording, the roots aphorism and the documented Todtnauberg home invitation are specific to Beaufret, not to Jaspers, Arendt, Löwith, Ortega y Gasset or Sartre’s 1930s exchange. The date variants 1945/1946/1948 are editorial/redactional noise around the same episode.

On the textual cluster plus the invitation sequel, Beaufret is the coherent and best-attested attribution.

